course: biology
lesson: 128
title: Marty, Gloria and Melman: real zebras, hippos and giraffes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Madagascar, Marty the zebra dreams of running free, Gloria the hippo floats gracefully, and Melman the giraffe worries about being ill, all after escaping from New York's Central Park Zoo.
explain: Marty asks whether he is white with black stripes or black with white stripes, and science has an answer: under the fur a zebra's skin is black, so it is black with white stripes.
explain: Gloria could not really float gracefully: a hippo's bones are so heavy that it sinks, and it walks and bounces along the river bottom, pushing up to breathe.
explain: Melman's long neck has only seven bones, the same number as yours, each one just very long, and his dark tongue, longer than a school ruler, pulls leaves past sharp thorns.
explain: On the real savanna zebras, giraffes and wildebeest graze side by side, eating different parts of the plants and watching out for lions together.
fact: Under its fur a zebra's skin is black, so scientists say zebras are black with white stripes, and every zebra's pattern is unique.
fact: The best-supported idea is that stripes keep away biting flies; in experiments, horses in striped coats got far fewer flies landing on them.
fact: Stripes might also confuse a lion trying to pick out one zebra from a running herd, but this is less certain.
fact: Zebras have never been tamed like horses; they are jumpy, kick hard and bite, and a zebra's kick can break a lion's jaw.
fact: Hippos cannot really swim or float; their heavy bones make them sink, so they walk along the riverbed and push up for air about every five minutes, even in their sleep, without waking.
fact: A hippo's skin makes an oily red-orange liquid, sometimes called blood sweat though it is neither blood nor sweat, which works like sunscreen.
fact: Hippos spend the day in water and come out at night to eat grass; their long tusks are for fighting, and they are among the most dangerous large animals in Africa.
fact: A hippo's closest living relatives are whales and dolphins, not pigs.
fact: A giraffe's neck has only seven bones, like almost all mammals, and its heart weighs about 11 kilograms to push blood up to its head.
fact: To drink, a giraffe must splay its front legs and bend low, so it drinks quickly, and it gets much of its water from leaves.
fact: A giraffe's dark tongue is about 45 to 50 centimetres long and pulls leaves past the thorns of acacia trees.
fact: The real Central Park Zoo is small and keeps penguins and sea lions, but not lions, zebras, hippos or giraffes.
fact: On the savanna, giraffes reach high leaves, zebras eat tough tall grass, and wildebeest eat the shorter grass left behind, and tall giraffes often spot lions first.
exercise: Is a zebra white with black stripes or black with white stripes? | Black with white stripes, since its skin is black | White with black stripes, since its skin is white | Grey with no real stripes | It changes colour in the sun
exercise: What do scientists think zebra stripes mostly do? | Keep away biting flies | Keep zebras cool in the snow | Help zebras hide in long green grass | Help zebras find water holes
exercise: What did experiments with horses in striped coats show? | Far fewer flies landed on them | They ran much faster | Lions were more scared of them | They got warmer at night
exercise: Why have zebras never been tamed like horses? | They are jumpy, kick hard and bite | They are too small and weak for a grown person to ride | They cannot eat hay | They sleep all day
exercise: Can a real hippo float and swim gracefully? | No, it sinks and walks along the river bottom | Yes, it floats like a duck on a pond and paddles gently | Yes, but only on its back | Yes, it swims faster than a dolphin
exercise: Why does a hippo sink in water? | Its bones are heavy and dense | It swallows lots of river water | It has no lungs to fill with air | Its skin is made of heavy stone
exercise: What is the red oily liquid on a hippo's skin? | A natural sunscreen, neither blood nor sweat | Real blood leaking from cuts all over its skin | Sweat from running | Mud mixed with berries
exercise: What does a hippo use its huge tusks for? | Fighting | Chewing grass | Digging for roots | Picking fruit
exercise: Which animals are a hippo's closest living relatives? | Whales and dolphins | Pigs and warthogs | Horses and zebras | Elephants and rhinos
exercise: How many bones are in a giraffe's neck? | Seven | Seventy | Twenty-five | Only two
exercise: Why does a giraffe need a heart of about 11 kilograms? | To push blood up to its head far above | To keep it warm at night | To help it run faster than the lions on the savanna | To store water for the desert
exercise: Why is drinking risky for a giraffe? | It must splay its legs and bend low, so lions can attack | Water makes its long neck far too heavy to lift up again | It cannot see water from up high | It has to stand in deep rivers
exercise: How long is a giraffe's tongue? | About 45 to 50 centimetres | About 5 centimetres | About 2 metres | About 15 centimetres
exercise: What does the giraffe do with its long dark tongue? | Pulls leaves past the long thorns of acacia trees | Catches flying insects out of the air like a frog | Licks salt off rocks only | Cleans its babies' ears
exercise: Which animals does the real Central Park Zoo keep? | Penguins and sea lions | Lions and striped zebras | Hippos and tall giraffes | Elephants and white rhinos
exercise: Why can zebras, giraffes and wildebeest share the same place? | They eat different parts of the plants | They take turns eating on different days | They all eat only leaves | They are all the same kind of animal
test: «zebras are white horses with black paint basically» What is right? | Zebras have black skin and are not tame horses | Right, they are just white horses painted with black stripes by nature | Right, any horse can become a zebra | Wrong, zebras are a kind of cow
test: «Gloria floats like a ballerina, so hippos are great swimmers» What is right? | Real hippos sink and bounce along the river bottom | Right, they swim like seals, twisting and diving with ease | Right, they float like balloons | Right, they glide like swans
test: «hippos look chill, can we paddle next to one» What is the best answer? | No, hippos are among the most dangerous large animals in Africa | Yes, they are gentle grass eaters that never hurt anybody | Yes, if you bring them carrots | Yes, they are slow and sleepy
test: «2 giraffes + 3 zebras, how many legs» How many? | 20 | 10 | 12 | 24
test: «3 giraffes, how many neck bones altogether, it's like a million» How many? | 21 | 3 | 30 | 210
test: «hippo's sweating blood, is it hurt??» What is right? | It is a red oily sunscreen, neither blood nor sweat | Yes, call a vet at once, it is bleeding from a cut | Yes, it was in a fight | Yes, hippos bleed when hot
test: «Melman's from Central Park Zoo so real giraffes live there» What is right? | The real Central Park Zoo is small and keeps no giraffes | Right, it has twenty giraffes living there, the biggest herd in America | Right, they roam the park | Right, all zoos have giraffes
test: What would happen if a lion tried to grab a zebra from behind? | It could get a kick hard enough to break its jaw | The zebra would lie down | The zebra would climb up a tree to get away from it | The lion would always win easily
test: Why might zebras like to graze near giraffes? | Giraffes are tall and spot lions early | Giraffes share their leaves with them | Giraffes scare all flies away | Giraffes carry zebras on their backs
test: «does a hippo drown when it falls asleep under water?» What is right? | No, it can come up for air while asleep without waking | Yes, hippos often drown if they fall asleep in deep water | No, hippos never sleep | No, hippos breathe water
test: «all zebras look the same, how do they tell each other apart lol» What is right? | Every zebra's stripe pattern is unique | They wear little name tags clipped to their ears | They cannot tell each other apart at all | Only the colour of their eyes is different
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colouration of the zebra. | The skin of the zebra is black, and the animal is accordingly described as black with white stripes | The skin of the zebra is white, and the black stripes are deposited upon the hair alone | The zebra possesses no stripes beneath its outer coat of hair | The colouration of the zebra alters according to exposure to sunlight
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the hippopotamus does not float. | Its bones are of great density and weight | It ingests a large quantity of water | It is devoid of lungs | Its skin is composed of mineral matter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the neck of the giraffe? | It contains seven bones, in common with almost all mammals, each being of great length | It contains approximately seventy bones, each being of the ordinary length found in other mammals | It contains no bones, being composed of muscle | It contains twice the number of bones found in the human neck
exam: Ascertain the explanation of zebra stripes which is at present best supported by experiment. | The stripes discourage biting flies from alighting | The stripes afford concealment in green vegetation | The stripes serve to retain heat in cold weather | The stripes assist the zebra in the location of water
